# ---------------------------------------------------------------- E.5 labelled liquid
R("T4 thus stands as a **measured null on this proxy**, with the filled-cup scene the decisive test — one that additionally needs a **cup-competent policy**: attempting it directly, our box-carry checkpoint did not transfer, completing 0/4 mug carries.",
  "T4 thus stands as a **measured null on this proxy**, with the filled-cup scene the decisive test — one that additionally needs a **cup-competent policy**: attempting it directly, our box-carry checkpoint did not transfer, completing 0/4 mug carries.\n\n**Labelled liquid (2026-09).** Since the checkpoint cannot carry an open cup, we asked whether telling it the payload is liquid changes how it holds the box: the same carry with \"the cup of water\" in the instruction, and with \"… Keep the cup level so the water does not spill\" appended (" + N['g_t3_seeds'] + ", 12 episodes each, scored with the method above). " + N['e5_text'] + " The tabletop family supplies the load that can spill: π0.5 carrying a mug (Appendix E.8).")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- E.8 tabletop family (replace the first paragraph and the T2 paragraph; keep the T1 paragraph)
R("### E.8 Cross-policy and cross-embodiment: the benchmark runs beyond GR00T-on-G1", "### E.8 Tabletop family: the sub-types on a Franka arm")
R("Everything above measures a single policy (GR00T N1.6) on a single embodiment (the G1 humanoid) — the honest limit we flag throughout. To show the taxonomy is not an artifact of one system, we port **two channels — the path/keep-out channel (T1) and the body-swept-volume channel (T2) — to a second, independently-trained VLA, π0.5 (openpi), driving a second embodiment, a Franka Panda arm** (the DROID configuration), on a tabletop pick-and-place task. Both metrics port with essentially no change: the T2 link-clearance predicate reduces `robot.data.body_pos_w`, which is embodiment-agnostic, so it applies directly to the Panda's links (`panda_link0…8` plus the gripper); the T1 keep-out predicate reuses the *very same* carried-object clearance metric we run on the G1, now tracking the Franka's manipulated object. π0.5 completes the task reliably (success = 100 % over the runs reported here), so — as with the G1 (§5.1) — we report over all episodes.",
  N['e8_intro'])
R("**T2 (body-sweep): the scoring geometry, not the policy, sets the rate.** With a bystander beside the workspace at four positions (*N* = 8 each), the **horizontal** metric",
  N['e8_body'] + "\n\n**T2, first probe: the scoring geometry, not the policy, sets the rate.** With an unrendered bystander at four positions inside the table footprint (*N* = 8 each), the **horizontal** metric")
R("We are careful about scope. π0.5 is a **stochastic flow-matching policy** (its actions vary run-to-run even at a fixed environment seed), the sample is small (*N* = 8 per position), and the task differs (a tabletop pick versus a loco-manipulation carry) — so this is a demonstration of **benchmark portability**, not a matched head-to-head ranking. What it removes is the \"single-policy artifact\" reading: the execution-phase body-sweep hazard recurs across a change of policy *and* embodiment (π0.5's arm touches the bystander's body surface on 25 % of episodes), and — as the threshold curve above shows — the benchmark also surfaces *how* the channel must be measured for the comparison to mean anything.",
  "We are careful about scope. π0.5 and π0 are **stochastic flow-matching policies** (their actions vary run-to-run even at a fixed environment seed), the cells are small (*N* = 8), and the task differs (a tabletop pick versus a loco-manipulation carry) — so this is **benchmark portability**, not a matched head-to-head ranking. That first probe placed the body where the arm works, inside the table footprint, where no one stands; the rendered adult at the table replaces it in Table III, and the probe is kept for what it shows about the metric: the threshold curve, not one margin, has to be reported.")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Appendix F
R("- **Proxies and witnesses by sub-type.** T3 uses a box's long axis as the hazardous axis, so its 52 % locates where a frozen axis falls rather than a validated blade-orientation harm; T4 is a null on a rigid box, whose level carry is trained task competence, so it cannot yet separate safety from capability (the clean test needs a load that can spill while the box is still delivered, and the checkpoint cannot carry an open cup, 0/4);",
  "- **Proxies and witnesses by sub-type.** On GR00T, T3 uses a box's long axis as the hazardous axis and T4 a rigid box that cannot spill (the checkpoint cannot carry an open cup, 0/4); the tabletop family replaces both with scissors and a mug" + N['appF_tt'] + ";")
